  "subtype": "success",
  "final_text": "I submitted **holistic** with confidence 0.45. The evidence is weak, so this is a lean, not a firm finding.\n\n**Design:**\n1. **Phase 1:** I trained `L1-S1-M1`, `L2-S2-M2` and `L3-S3-M3`. The main test task was `L3-S1-M2`: all three of its rules had been trained, but its mapping matched no trained task on more than 37.5% of inputs. I compared it with two others:\n   - `L1-S1-M4`: one new rule, 75% match with a trained task.\n   - `L4-S4-M4`: three new rules, at most 19% match.\n2. **Phases 2 and 3:** I trained three more tasks, then three more, so that each of the test task's rules appeared in 2 and then 3 trained tasks. This covers both possible values of k. The test task's best match with any trained task stayed at 37.5% throughout.\n\n**Results** (examples needed to reach criterion, averaged over repeated tests):\n\n| Phase | Task | Tests | Average |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `L1-S1-M4` (1 new rule, most similar) | 6 | ~22 |\n| 1 | `L3-S1-M2` (0 new rules) | 6 | ~26 |\n| 1 | `L4-S4-M4` (3 new rules, least similar) | 6 | ~30 |\n| 2 (rules in 2 tasks) | `L3-S1-M2` | 10 | ~24 |\n| 3 (rules in 3 tasks) | `L3-S1-M2` | 6 | ~27 |\n\n- **Phase 1:** the order follows similarity rather than how many rules were new. A part-based learner should have found `L3-S1-M2` the cheapest.\n- **Phases 2 and 3:** the test task never got cheaper once its rules had appeared in 2 or 3 trained tasks. That argues against factor-when-justified with either k.\n\n**Why confidence is low:** the noise was huge compared with any effect. A task the learner had already been trained on took anywhere from 5 to 37 examples, about 22 on average, which is no cheaper than the new tasks. So every difference above is small next to the spread, and \"cannot decide\" would also have been defensible.\n\nI used 53 of the 60 budget: 9 on training and 44 on tests.",
